A||||5||dwilsonfl||brake valve nuts removal||||5||
Z||000000||dwilsonfl||12-12-2001||01:07 AM||||How do I remove these nuts? I can't budge them. is there a trick?||||reg||5
Z||000001||honegod||12-12-2001||04:03 AM||||be prepared to buy new calipers, seriously.<BR>I presume you are talking about the bleeder valves. they are fragile and get SERIOUSLY stuck in place. the odds are great that it will require more torque to unstick them than it takes to tear them in half.<P>sorry, I have no sure fire technique that will work to offer.<P>the best advice is to attack the problem KNOWING that replacement is likely, that way you will feel free to try drastic measures that might work, or destroy the caliper.<BR>fearlessly. ||||reg||9
Z||000002||fierohoho||12-12-2001||04:51 AM||||I was able to break loose my bleeders doing the following.<P>First I used a drill bit "by hand" to clean the center of the bleeder of any foriegn material, then I cleaned around the exposed threads with a small wire brush, I then filled the center with liquid wrench and wet down the threads, I let this sit overnight and repeated the process the next two days.<P>Tapping on the bleeder for a couple minutes after each dousing sets up vibrations which help the liquid wrench penetrate better.<P>After the three days I used a 6 point socket and a small breaker bar to first tighten the bleeders a "hair" then to remove them.<P>I was able to remove all 4 without any breakage, I then wire brushed the all to clean them up and reinstalled them.<P>I always make sure I have the rubber caps on them to keep the dirt out of the center and about once a year I crack them loose to keep them from freezing again.<P>Good luck.<P>------------------<BR>'84 SE 2.5l<BR>'85 Coupe 2.5l now getting a 3800<BR>'86 SE 2.5l<BR>       <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/lightbar.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000003||HummelHund||12-13-2001||01:19 PM||||Nope, I'm talking about the nuts on the porportioning valve under the master cylinder. Can't budge them at all, and I need to replace the brake lines.||||reg||1
Z||000004||honegod||12-13-2001||08:06 PM||||well then, your calipers sound safe  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>there are special wrenches made for nuts with pipes coming out the middle, like the brake line nuts I now presume you are talking about.<BR>it looks like a box end wrench with a chunk cut out of it to let it get past the pipe.<BR>the idea being to use more than two flats for torque application.<P>keep in mind that the nuts are not too TIGHT, they are STUCK. that means that they can be tightened to break them loose as fierohoho talked about above in his excellent procedure. the patient application of liquid wrench with a gentle working of the wrench back and forth to flex the nut a little to help the liquid wrench work its way in.||||reg||1
Z||000005||theogre||12-13-2001||08:49 PM||||You need a set of metric flair nut wrenches. don't even mess around with anything else on these.<P>If the lines will not come out of the block, even after using penetrating oil....<P>use a PROPANE torch to heat the combination valve right by the fitting. Load the nut with a wrench while you heat the combi valve with a LOW FLAME. You just want to warm the thing up. Not melt it or its guts. Keep the flame off the nut.<P>If you do damage the combi valve then you'll have to dig up a spare from a salvage yard or see if the parts stores have them. (I'd check parts stores before trying any heat.) <P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/fullstaff.gif">   <FONT COLOR="blue"><I><B>11-Sept-01, The day the world as we knew it ended.</B></I></FONT><p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 12-13-2001).]||||reg||1
